Implementing a multi-tree list within an Angular application involves creating two separate components: one for the main tree and another for the secondary (or tertiary) tree. Each component will handle its own data and interactions. Below is a step-by-step guide on how to achieve this:

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, ensure you have Node.js installed. Then, create a new Angular project if you don't already have one.

```bash
ng new multiTreeListApp
cd multiTreeListApp
```

### Step 2: Create Components

Create two Angular components, `MultiTreeComponent` and `SecondaryTreeComponent`.

#### MultiTreeComponent

This component will display the main tree structure.

**multi-tree.component.ts**
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-multi-tree',
  templateUrl: './multi-tree.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./multi-tree.component.css']
})
export class MultiTreeComponent {
  // Example data for demonstration purposes
  public nodes = [
    {
      id: 0,
      name: 'Root',
      children: [
        { id: 1, name: 'Child 1' },
        { id: 2, name: 'Child 2' }
      ]
    }
  ];

  addNode(parentId: number): void {
    const newNode = { id: Math.random(), name: 'New Child', parentId };
    const parentIndex = this.nodes.findIndex(node => node.id === parentId);
    if (parentIndex !== -1) {
      this.nodes[parentIndex].children?.push(newNode);
    }
  }

  removeNode(id: number): void {
    const index = this.nodes.findIndex(node => node.id === id);
    if (index !== -1) {
      this.nodes.splice(index, 1);
    }
  }
}
```

**multi-tree.component.html**
```html
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let node of nodes">
    {{node.name}}
    <button (click)="addNode(node.id)">Add Child</button>
    <ul *ngIf="node.children && node.children.length > 0">
      <li *ngFor="let child of node.children">
        {{child.name}}<button (click)="removeNode(child.id)">Remove</button>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>
```

